<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Davenport,_Iowa> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Davenport** is a city in and the county seat of [Scott County](:Scott_County%2C_Iowa), [Iowa](:Iowa), United States. Located along the [Mississippi River](:Mississippi_River) on the eastern border of the state, it is the largest of the [Quad Cities](:Quad_Cities), a [metropolitan area](:Metropolitan_statistical_area) with a population of 384,324 and a [combined statistical area](:List_of_Combined_Statistical_Areas) population of 474,019, ranking as the 147th-largest MSA and 91st-largest CSA in the nation. According to the [2020 census](:2020_United_States_Census), the city had a population of 101,724, making it Iowa's third-largest city. Davenport was founded on May 14, 1836, by [Antoine Le Claire](:Antoine_Le_Claire) and was named for his friend [George Davenport](:George_Davenport), a former English sailor who served in the U.S. Army during the [War of 1812](:War_of_1812), served as a supplier [Fort Armstrong](:Fort_Armstrong%2C_Illinois), worked as a fur trader with the [American Fur Company](:American_Fur_Company), and was appointed a quartermaster with the rank of colonel during the [Black Hawk War](:Black_Hawk_War).The city is prone to frequent flooding due to its location on the Mississippi River. There are two main universities: [St. Ambrose University](:St%2E_Ambrose_University) and [Palmer College of Chiropractic](:Palmer_College_of_Chiropractic), where the first chiropractic adjustment took place. Several annual music festivals take place in Davenport, including the Mississippi Valley Blues Festival, the Mississippi Valley Fair, and the [Bix Beiderbecke Memorial Jazz Festival](:Bix_Beiderbecke_Memorial_Jazz_Festival). An internationally known 7-mile (11 km) foot race, called the [Bix 7](:Bix_7), is run during the festival. The city has a Class A minor-league baseball team, the [Quad Cities River Bandits](:Quad_Cities_River_Bandits). Davenport has 50 plus parks and facilities, as well as more than 20 miles (32 km) of recreational paths for biking or walking.Three [interstates](:Interstate_Highway_system) (80, 74 and 280) and two major [United States Highways](:United_States_Numbered_Highways) serve the city. Davenport has seen steady population growth since its incorporation. National economic difficulties in the 1980s resulted in job and population losses. The Quad Cities was ranked as the most affordable metropolitan area in 2010 by *[Forbes](:Forbes)* magazine. In 2007, Davenport, along with neighboring Rock Island, won the City Livability Award in the small-city category from the [U.S. Conference of Mayors](:U%2ES%2E_Conference_of_Mayors). In 2012, Davenport, and the [Quad Cities Metropolitan Area](:Quad_Cities_Metropolitan_Area), was ranked among the fastest-growing areas in the nation in the growth of high-tech jobs. Notable natives of the city have included jazz legend [Bix Beiderbecke](:Bix_Beiderbecke), Pulitzer Prize-winning playwright [Susan Glaspell](:Susan_Glaspell), former [National Football League](:National_Football_League) [running back](:Running_back) [Roger Craig](:Roger_Craig_%28American_football%29), UFC Welterweight Champion [Pat Miletich](:Pat_Miletich), IBF Middleweight and WBA Super Middleweight boxing champion [Michael Nunn](:Michael_Nunn), and former two-time [WWE Champion](:WWE_Championship) and [WWE Universal Champion](:WWE_Universal_Championship) [Seth Rollins](:Seth_Rollins)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
