<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Buffalo,_New_York> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Buffalo** is the [second-largest city](:List_of_cities_in_New_York) in the U.S. state of [New York](:New_York_%28state%29) (behind only [New York City](:New_York_City%29) and the seat of [Erie County](:Erie_County%2C_New_York). It is at the eastern end of [Lake Erie](:Lake_Erie), at the head of the [Niagara River](:Niagara_River), and is across the [Canadian border](:Canada%E2%80%93United_States_border) from [Southern Ontario](:Southern_Ontario). With a population of 278,349 according to the 2020 census, Buffalo is the 78th-largest city in the United States. The city and nearby [Niagara Falls](:Niagara_Falls%2C_New_York) together make up the two-county [Buffalo–Niagara Falls Metropolitan Statistical Area (MSA)](:Buffalo%E2%80%93Niagara_Falls_metropolitan_area), which had an estimated population of 1.1 million in 2020, making it the [49th largest MSA](:List_of_metropolitan_statistical_areas) in the United States. Buffalo is in [Western New York](:Western_New_York), which is the largest population and economic center between [Boston](:Boston) and [Cleveland](:Cleveland).Before the 17th century, the region was inhabited by nomadic [Paleo-Indians](:Paleo-Indians) who were succeeded by the [Neutral](:Neutral_Nation), [Erie](:Erie_people), and [Iroquois](:Iroquois) nations. In the early 17th century, the French began to explore the region. In the 18th century, Iroquois land surrounding [Buffalo Creek](:Buffalo_River_%28New_York%29) was ceded through the [Holland Land Purchase](:Holland_Land_Purchase), and a small village was established at its headwaters. In 1825, after its harbor was improved, Buffalo was selected as the terminus of the [Erie Canal](:Erie_Canal), which led to its incorporation in 1832. The canal stimulated its growth as the primary [inland port](:Inland_port) between the Great Lakes and the Atlantic Ocean. [Transshipment](:Transshipment) made Buffalo the world's largest [grain port](:Grain_trade) of that era. After the coming of railroads reduced the canal's importance, the city became the second-largest railway hub (after Chicago). During the mid-19th century, Buffalo transitioned to manufacturing, which came to be dominated by steel production. Later, [deindustrialization](:Deindustrialization) and the opening of the [St. Lawrence Seaway](:St%2E_Lawrence_Seaway) saw the city's economy decline and diversify. It developed its [service industries](:Service_industries), such as health care, retail, tourism, logistics, and education, while retaining some manufacturing. In 2019, the [gross domestic product](:Gross_domestic_product) of the Buffalo–Niagara Falls MSA was $53 billion.The city's cultural landmarks include the [oldest urban parks system](:Parks_and_recreation_in_Buffalo%2C_New_York) in the United States, the [Albright–Knox Art Gallery](:Albright%E2%80%93Knox_Art_Gallery), the [Buffalo Philharmonic Orchestra](:Buffalo_Philharmonic_Orchestra), [Shea's Performing Arts Center](:Shea%27s_Performing_Arts_Center), the [Buffalo Museum of Science](:Buffalo_Museum_of_Science), and several [annual festivals](:List_of_festivals_in_Buffalo%2C_New_York). Its educational institutions include the [University at Buffalo](:University_at_Buffalo), [Buffalo State College](:Buffalo_State_College), [Canisius College](:Canisius_College), [D'Youville University](:D%27Youville_University) and [Medaille College](:Medaille_College). Buffalo is also known for its [winter weather](:Lake-effect_snow), [Buffalo wings](:Buffalo_wing), and two major-league [sports teams](:Sports_in_Buffalo): the National Football League's [Buffalo Bills](:Buffalo_Bills) and the National Hockey League's [Buffalo Sabres](:Buffalo_Sabres)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
