<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Cleveland> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Cleveland** ( [*KLEEV-lənd*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29), officially the **City of Cleveland**, is a city in the [U.S.](:United_States) [state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Ohio](:Ohio) and the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Cuyahoga County](:Cuyahoga_County%2C_Ohio). Located in the northeastern part of the state, it is situated along the southern shore of [Lake Erie](:Lake_Erie), across the U.S. [maritime border](:Canada%E2%80%93United_States_border) with [Canada](:Canada) and approximately 60 miles (100 kilometers) west of [Pennsylvania](:Pennsylvania).The largest city on Lake Erie and one of the major cities of the [Great Lakes region](:Great_Lakes_region), Cleveland ranks as the [54th-largest city](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) in the U.S. with a 2020 population of 372,624. The city anchors both the [Greater Cleveland metropolitan statistical area](:Greater_Cleveland) (MSA) and the larger [Cleveland–Akron–Canton combined statistical area](:Northeast_Ohio) (CSA). The CSA is the most populous in Ohio and the 17th largest in the country, with a population of 3.63 million in 2020, while the MSA ranks as [34th largest](:Metropolitan_statistical_area) at 2.09 million.Cleveland was founded in 1796 near the mouth of the [Cuyahoga River](:Cuyahoga_River) by General [Moses Cleaveland](:Moses_Cleaveland), after whom the city was named. Its location on both the river and the lake shore allowed it to grow into a major commercial and industrial center, attracting large numbers of [immigrants](:Immigration_to_the_United_States) and [migrants](:Great_Migration_%28African_American%29). A [port city](:Port_of_Cleveland), Cleveland is connected to the [Atlantic Ocean](:Atlantic_Ocean) via the [Saint Lawrence Seaway](:Saint_Lawrence_Seaway). Its [economy](:Economy_of_Greater_Cleveland) relies on diversified sectors such as manufacturing, financial services, [healthcare](:Health_care_in_the_United_States), biomedicals, and higher education. The [GDP](:Gross_domestic_product) for the Greater Cleveland MSA was $135 billion in 2019. Combined with the [Akron MSA](:Akron_metropolitan_area), the seven-county Cleveland–Akron metropolitan economy was $175 billion in 2019, the largest in Ohio, accounting for 25% of the state's GDP.Designated as a \"Gamma -\" [global city](:Global_city) by the [Globalization and World Cities Research Network](:Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network), Cleveland is home to several major cultural institutions, including the [Cleveland Museum of Art](:Cleveland_Museum_of_Art), the [Cleveland Museum of Natural History](:Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History), the [Cleveland Orchestra](:Cleveland_Orchestra), [Playhouse Square](:Playhouse_Square), and the [Rock and Roll Hall of Fame](:Rock_and_Roll_Hall_of_Fame). Known as \"[The Forest City](:The_Forest_City)\" among many other [nicknames](:Nicknames_of_Cleveland), Cleveland serves as the center of the [Cleveland Metroparks](:Cleveland_Metroparks) nature reserve system. The city's [major league](:Major_professional_sports_leagues_in_the_United_States_and_Canada) [professional sports teams](:Sports_in_Cleveland) include the [Cleveland Browns](:Cleveland_Browns), the [Cleveland Cavaliers](:Cleveland_Cavaliers), and the [Cleveland Guardians](:Cleveland_Guardians)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
