<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Winnipeg> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Winnipeg** ( ) is the capital and largest city of the province of [Manitoba](:Manitoba) in Canada. It is centred on the [confluence](:Confluence) of the [Red](:Red_River_of_the_North) and [Assiniboine](:Assiniboine_River) rivers, near the [longitudinal centre](:Longitude) of North America. As of 2021[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Winnipeg&action=edit), Winnipeg had a city population of 749,607 and a metropolitan population of 834,678, making it the [sixth-largest](:List_of_the_largest_municipalities_in_Canada_by_population) city, and [eighth-largest](:List_of_census_metropolitan_areas_and_agglomerations_in_Canada) metropolitan area in Canada.The city is named after the nearby [Lake Winnipeg](:Lake_Winnipeg); the name comes from the [Western Cree](:Cree_language) words for \"muddy water\" - “winipīhk”. The region was a trading centre for [Indigenous peoples](:Indigenous_peoples_in_Canada) long before the arrival of Europeans; it is the traditional territory of the Anishinabe (Ojibway), Ininew (Cree), Oji-Cree, Dene, and Dakota, and is the birthplace of the [Métis Nation](:M%C3%A9tis_people_in_Canada). French traders built the first fort on the site in 1738. A settlement was later founded by the [Selkirk settlers](:Red_River_Colony) of the Red River Colony in 1812, the nucleus of which was incorporated as the City of Winnipeg in 1873. Being far inland, the [local climate](:Winnipeg%23Climate) is extremely seasonal even by Canadian standards with average January highs of around −11 °C (12 °F) and average July highs of 26 °C (79 °F).Known as the \"Gateway to the West\", Winnipeg is a railway and transportation hub with a diversified economy. This multicultural city hosts numerous annual festivals, including the [Festival du Voyageur](:Festival_du_Voyageur), the [Winnipeg Folk Festival](:Winnipeg_Folk_Festival), the [Jazz Winnipeg Festival](:Jazz_Winnipeg_Festival), the [Winnipeg Fringe Theatre Festival](:Winnipeg_Fringe_Theatre_Festival), and [Folklorama](:Folklorama). In [1967](:1967_Pan_American_Games), Winnipeg was the first Canadian host of the [Pan American Games](:Pan_American_Games). It is home to several professional sports franchises, including the [Winnipeg Blue Bombers](:Winnipeg_Blue_Bombers) ([Canadian football](:Canadian_football%29), the [Winnipeg Jets](:Winnipeg_Jets) ([ice hockey](:Ice_hockey%29), [Manitoba Moose](:Manitoba_Moose) (ice hockey), [Valour FC](:Valour_FC) ([association football](:Association_football%29), and the [Winnipeg Goldeyes](:Winnipeg_Goldeyes) ([baseball](:Baseball%29)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
