<http://dbpedia.org/resource/North_American_Soccer_League_(2011–2017)> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Saskatoon> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Saskatoon** () is the largest city in the [Canadian province](:Provinces_and_territories_of_Canada) of [Saskatchewan](:Saskatchewan). It straddles a bend in the [South Saskatchewan River](:South_Saskatchewan_River) in the central region of the province. It is located along the [Trans-Canada](:Trans-Canada_Highway) [Yellowhead Highway](:Yellowhead_Highway), and has served as the cultural and economic hub of central Saskatchewan since its founding in 1882 as a [Temperance](:Temperance_movement) colony.With a [2021 census population](:Canada_2021_Census) of 266,141, Saskatoon is the [largest city in the province](:List_of_cities_in_Saskatchewan), and the [17th largest Census Metropolitan Area in Canada](:List_of_census_metropolitan_areas_and_agglomerations_in_Canada), with a 2021 census population of 317,480.Saskatoon is home to the [University of Saskatchewan](:University_of_Saskatchewan), the [Meewasin Valley Authority](:Meewasin_Valley_Authority) (which protects the South Saskatchewan River and provides for the city's popular riverbank park spaces), and [Wanuskewin Heritage Park](:Wanuskewin_Heritage_Park) (a [National Historic Site of Canada](:National_Historic_Site_of_Canada) and [UNESCO](:UNESCO) World Heritage applicant representing 6,000 years of [First Nations](:First_Nations_in_Canada) history). The [Rural Municipality of Corman Park No. 344](:Corman_Park_No%2E_344%2C_Saskatchewan), the most populous [rural municipality](:List_of_rural_municipalities_in_Saskatchewan) in Saskatchewan, surrounds the city and contains many of the developments associated with it, including Wanuskewin.Saskatoon is named after the [saskatoon berry](:Amelanchier_alnifolia) which is native to the region, and is itself derived from the [Cree](:Cree_language) **misâskwatômina**. The city has a significant [Indigenous](:First_Nations_in_Canada) population and several urban [Reserves](:Indian_reserve). The city has nine river crossings and is [nicknamed](:List_of_city_nicknames_and_slogans_in_Canada%23Saskatchewan) \"Paris of the Prairies\" and \"Bridge City\".Historic neighbourhoods of Saskatoon include [Nutana](:Nutana) and [Riversdale](:Riversdale%2C_Saskatoon), which were separate towns before amalgamating with the town of Saskatoon and incorporating as a city in 1906. Nutana, Riversdale, their historic main streets of [Broadway Avenue](:Broadway_Avenue_%28Saskatoon%29) and [20th Street](:Riversdale%2C_Saskatoon%23Commercial), as well as the [downtown core](:Central_Business_District%2C_Saskatoon) and other central neighbourhoods are seeing significant reinvestment and redevelopment. [Sutherland](:Sutherland%2C_Saskatoon), the rail town annexed by the city in 1956 that lies beyond the [University](:University_of_Saskatchewan) lands, is now another historic old city."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
